"""The French Revolution, which began in 1789, was one of the most significant political upheavals in modern history. It emerged from a combination of financial crisis, social inequality, and the spread of Enlightenment ideas. By the late 1780s, France was deeply in debt, largely due to its involvement in costly wars, including support for the American Revolution. At the same time, the rigid social structure placed the heaviest tax burden on the Third Estate, while the clergy and nobility enjoyed significant privileges.

When King Louis XVI called the Estates-General in 1789, tensions quickly came to a head. Representatives of the Third Estate declared themselves the National Assembly and vowed to create a new constitution. The storming of the Bastille on July 14 became a powerful symbol of resistance against royal authority.

The revolution ultimately reshaped France and inspired movements around the world. While it led to periods of violence and instability, it also introduced lasting ideas about citizenship, equality, and the rights of individuals.""",
"""Photosynthesis is the process by which plants, algae, and some bacteria convert light energy into chemical energy. It takes place primarily in the chloroplasts, which contain a green pigment called chlorophyll. This pigment absorbs sunlight, mainly in the blue and red wavelengths, and uses that energy to drive a series of chemical reactions.

The process can be divided into two main stages. In the light-dependent reactions, which occur in the thylakoid membranes, water molecules are split, releasing oxygen as a byproduct. This stage also produces ATP and NADPH, which serve as energy carriers. In the second stage, known as the Calvin cycle, these energy carriers are used to convert carbon dioxide from the air into glucose.

Photosynthesis is essential for life on Earth. Not only does it provide the oxygen we breathe, but it also forms the foundation of nearly every food chain.""",
