"""Great question! The main difference between weather and climate comes down to time. Weather describes the short-term conditions of the atmosphere at a specific place and time, such as whether it's sunny, rainy, or windy today. It can change from hour to hour or day to day.

Climate, on the other hand, refers to the average weather patterns in a region over a long period, usually 30 years or more. For example, a desert has a hot and dry climate, even though it might occasionally rain there.

A simple way to remember it is this: weather is what you get on any given day, while climate is what you expect over the long run.""",
