"""Sure! Here's a quick overview of how a bill becomes a law in the United States. First, a member of Congress introduces the bill in either the House of Representatives or the Senate. The bill is then sent to a committee, where it is studied, discussed, and sometimes changed.

If the committee approves the bill, it goes to the full chamber for debate and a vote. If it passes, the bill moves to the other chamber, where the same process happens again. Both chambers must agree on the exact same version of the bill.

Once that happens, the bill is sent to the President, who can either sign it into law or veto it. If the President vetoes the bill, Congress can still override the veto with a two-thirds vote in both chambers.""",
